  {
   "id": "obstructed",
   "status": "arrest",
   "_rhythm_note": "PEA. Clinical judgement, confirmed by the producer 2026-08-29, and going to the clinical reviewer as a confirmation rather than a blocker. A five-year-old who arrests behind a swollen epiglottis arrests from asphyxia, and asphyxial paediatric arrest is bradyasystolic, never VF: the reflex being taught is oxygen and a surgical airway, not a shock. PEA and asystole are both non-shockable, so the defibrillator and the mark sheet behave identically either way. PEA is declared because the engine holds the heart rate through an arrest and this case leaves it at 148, and the word asystole beside a rate of 148 is the same impossible reading physiologic_plausibility.rs exists to catch. To declare asystole instead, the case must first drive the rate down.",
   "rhythm": "pea",
